<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">New Hampshire Grants</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1749">1749</a>–<a href="/years/1764">1764</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-hampshire">New Hampshire</a> Grants were land grants made between 1749 and 1764 by Governor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/benning-wentworth">Benning Wentworth</a> in the territory west of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/connecticut-river">Connecticut River</a>, which <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-york">New York</a> also claimed. In 1764 the Crown ruled for New York, leading to conflict with the settlers, who organised the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/green-mountain-boys">Green Mountain Boys</a> under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ethan-allen">Ethan Allen</a>. The settlers declared the independent Vermont Republic in 1777.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/new-hampshire-grants.jpg" alt="New Hampshire Grants" width="320" height="473" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Thomas Jefferys; derivative work by Treehill (fr) Treehill · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:New_Hampshire_grants_(1776).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>The border between Massachusetts and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-hampshire">New Hampshire</a> was fixed by royal decree in 1741, and the same year <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/benning-wentworth">Benning Wentworth</a> became the first 18th-century governor of New Hampshire who was not also governor of Massachusetts. Wentworth read the decree to mean that New Hampshire reached as far west as Massachusetts did, to a line 20 miles east of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hudson-river">Hudson River</a>. New York claimed the same land, between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/connecticut-river">Connecticut River</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lake-champlain">Lake Champlain</a>, under the letters patent that had granted the Duke of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/york">York</a> all the lands west of the Connecticut River to Delaware Bay.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Wentworth made his first grant, Bennington, on 3 January 1749, and went on despite <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-york">New York</a>'s protests; after surveys in 1755 and 1757, 108 grants were made, reaching north to the eastern shore of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lake-champlain">Lake Champlain</a>. About 135 grants were made in all, including 131 towns. A grant was usually six miles square and cost 20 pounds, with lots set aside for the Society for the Propagation of the Gospel in Foreign Parts, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/church-of-england">Church of England</a>, the first clergyman, a school and two for Wentworth himself.</p><p>A royal order of 26 July 1764 made the western bank of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/connecticut">Connecticut</a> the boundary, and Wentworth issued his last two grants, Walker and Waltham, on 17 October. New York treated the order as voiding his grants, divided the territory into four counties and required grantees to surrender their charters, often buying their land back at much higher prices. In 1770 the New York Supreme Court declared all of Wentworth's grants invalid, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ethan-allen">Ethan Allen</a> and his <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/green-mountain-boys">Green Mountain Boys</a> rose to resist.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>In January 1775 committees of safety from over twenty towns met at Manchester to seek self-government, and on 13 March two men were killed at Westminster by officers from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-york">New York</a>. Vermont declared its independence in 1777, held its first elections on 3 March 1778 and stayed outside the confederation of the former colonies, mainly through New York's objections. In 1790 New York ceded its claim for 30,000 dollars, a convention at Bennington voted 105–2 for admission in January 1791, and on 4 March 1791 Vermont became the 14th state.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “New Hampshire Grants” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Settlers in the <b>New Hampshire Grants</b> declared independence as New Connecticut, renamed Vermont that summer.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/january-15">15 January in history</a></li><li><blockquote>Under Benning Wentworth, governor from 1741 to 1766, it issued the <b>New Hampshire Grants</b> west of the Connecticut River.</blockquote><a href="/state/province-of-new-hampshire">Province of New Hampshire</a></li></ul></section>
